# Copy to .env.production. `VITE_G2A_API_BASE` is the ONLY required value.
#
# It is the REST base of the g2a-business-api plugin, INCLUDING the
# /wp-json/g2a/v1 suffix (fixed auth contract: {API_BASE}/auth/session/...).
#
# USE THE RELATIVE PATH. Do not point this at https://guns2ammo.com/...
#
#   VITE_G2A_API_BASE=/wp-json/g2a/v1
#
# Why relative: authentication is an HttpOnly session cookie plus an
# X-G2A-CSRF header. app.guns2ammo.com serves the app and reverse-proxies
# /wp-json/g2a/v1/ to WordPress (deploy/nginx-app.guns2ammo.com.conf), so every
# API call is SAME-ORIGIN and the browser sends the session cookie
# automatically. The CSP in deploy/nginx-security-headers.conf pins
# connect-src to 'self', so an absolute base is blocked there too.
#
# An absolute cross-origin base breaks that: the cookie becomes third-party,
# needs SameSite=None plus credentialed CORS, and is dropped outright by
# browsers with third-party cookies blocked. The symptom is the login loop —
# sign in, land on the dashboard, get bounced back to login on the next
# request. deploy/deploy.sh builds with the relative base for this reason; a
# manual rebuild must match it.
#
# VITE_G2A_USE_MOCKS is IGNORED in production builds — mock mode is dev-only
# and the fixtures are not shipped in production bundles. It is pinned to 0
# here so a copied .env cannot re-enable it by accident.

VITE_G2A_API_BASE=/wp-json/g2a/v1
VITE_G2A_USE_MOCKS=0
